- op: discuss  # a7af
  at: '2026-04-15T08:47:06Z'  # a7af
  by: agent  # a7af
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a7af
  clock: 4  # a7af
  nonce: a7af  # a7af
  message: "Good question — yes, there is a clever short way. I had it framed wrong in my previous post; thanks for pushing.\n\n**The clever short way (Path B′):** ONE post-analysis pass that rewrites `calls→Class` edges to `calls→Class.method` when sibling evidence exists. Per-language analyzer edits are NOT required.\n\nMechanism:\n1. The analyzers we already have emit *some* signal about chained method calls. Two relevant signals already in the graph:\n   - The `calls→Class` edge (from constructor invocation `Class()`).\n   - The `unresolved_external_call` (or `unresolved-call`) edges that WI-banaf and WI-vurop just landed for JS/TS, plus the equivalents elsewhere — these carry the bare method name in their metadata (e.g., `name=run`).\n2. Post-pass logic at link/enrichment time, applied uniformly across all languages:\n   - For each node N with an outgoing `calls→Class` edge AND an outgoing `unresolved-call(name=foo)` edge,\n   - If `Class` has a contained child symbol named `foo` (via `contains` edges), rewrite `unresolved-call(foo)` to `calls→Class.foo`. Keep the `calls→Class` (constructor) edge.\n3. The slice naturally then includes `Class.foo` via the new direct edge — no fan-out heuristic needed in slice.py, no design tension with `test_forward_slice_class_reaches_method_then_no_sibling_explosion`.\n\nCoverage: works for any language whose analyzer emits both signals. JS/TS already does (post-banaf/vurop). Java, Kotlin, and Python emit similar partial-resolution edges via the existing `unresolved_external_call` / `unresolved-call` family (and we already match against catalogs in io-boundaries using these). I should grep the analyzers to enumerate which already emit the bare-name signal vs. which need a small additional emit; that's at worst a one-line addition per analyzer rather than a per-language type-inference effort.\n\nEdge cases worth naming up front:\n- **Method shadowing**: `Class.foo` exists but is overridden by an instance method on the runtime type (e.g., the actual instance is a subclass). Acceptable false positive — over-attribute to the parent's method, slice still gets useful nodes.\n- **Multiple matches**: `Class().run(...)` where two unresolved-call hints exist in the same caller. Disambiguate by line proximity (caller-line of the `Class()` instantiation should be close to the bare `run` call).\n- **Builder chains**: `new Foo().bar().baz()` — only the first method on the literal class is recoverable this way; `.baz()` requires knowing `bar()`'s return type. That's the hard typed-call-resolution problem; this post-pass intentionally doesn't try to solve it. Builders are a separate item, not WI-gigoz.\n\nRecommendation: do Path B′. Implementation surface is one new file (e.g. `linkers/method_call_recovery.py`), one wire-in spot, plus a regression test using the WI-gigoz CliRunner shape. Ballpark ~150 lines of code + tests, ~half a day. Substantially smaller than per-analyzer typed call resolution and naturally fixes WI-gigoz without touching slice.py at all.\n\nIf you greenlight, I'll pick this up next — happy to scope a tracker item with the per-analyzer signal audit as the first step."  # a7af
